diff --git a/broker/change/ASSUMPTIONS.md b/broker/change/ASSUMPTIONS.md
index fa0c97322..b92a6990b 100644
--- a/broker/change/ASSUMPTIONS.md
+++ b/broker/change/ASSUMPTIONS.md
@@ -25,3 +25,4 @@ Built for PLAN P3 ("change pipeline with held-out suites") against SPEC v0.12: C
 | C19 | **Deletion (CAP-3).** `ForgetTasks` removes the task cases built on journal intents that recall's deletion reach erased: their input and expected output may hold the deleted record. It is erasure, not a suite change, so it needs no owner intent (unlike CHG-2 removal); security fixtures have no task and are never removed this way. Threat: an agent able to delete sources could shrink the held-out set. It fails closed, since below `MinHeldOut` no candidate qualifies, and `state.Forgotten` counts the removals for the digest. In `agentosd` recall's `ServiceConfig.Cases` is the learning plane's `ForgetTasks`, one method so the parts cannot be wired apart (security F1 on #59): the harvester's tombstone first (`loops.Harvester.ForgetIntents`: `Harvest` refuses the intent from then on, so one still in flight cannot settle into a case; its `Added` mark goes, so evidence stops counting it; `Tasks` and `Origins` stay, so mining still skips it), then these cases, then the task values kept from the intents' params (an intent still queued for values when the forget runs is refused when it is drained, security F2 on #59). The reach runs it before the journal erases the intents and again after, and on every retry. **Who may delete (security A1 on #59):** the first production caller of `recall.Index.Delete` must be an authenticated owner deletion or a source deletion the owner confirmed, never an agent's request or a source-side sync an agent can trigger; otherwise an agent could erase its own lineage's Rejected cases. | CAP-3, CHG-1, CHG-2 | Show `Forgotten` in the digest. |
 | C20 | **New identifiers in context rules (W3-builder, security C-3c-4).** C6's new-identifier check also covers context rules, so it covers every text file a Loop 1 builder may write (procedures, skills, context). A context rule naming an address the active tree never used asks the owner. Its select list is already limited to sources the machine receives. | CHG-6 | — |
 | C21 | **Which interruptions count (PE5; arbitrator, with security F1 on #103 and P1 to P5).** Only a cut a candidate could have caused counts toward `MaxInterruptions` (still 2). The host marks the rest with errors wrapping `ErrOwnerPreempt`, each logged by a fixed class: `ErrOwnerStop` (STOP, LOOPS OFF, pausing the running loop; `owner-stop`); `ErrOwnerWork` (the owner's accepted work, `admission.Request.Owner`, while memory pressure is within its limit; `owner-work`); `ErrNoRoomPreempt` (admission has no room for the replay machine's fixed declared budget, while no other replay machine and no other running experiment holds room; `no-room`); `ErrClassRevoke` (admission recorded, when it picked the replay machine as victim, that it was for foreground or the owner's accepted work without pressure; `class-revoke`). Foreground is admitted whatever the pressure, so a revoke for foreground is exempt under pressure too: no candidate can start the owner's foreground work. The scheduler reads busy, owner and pressure in one `admission.BusyCause` call, and pressure wins. Accepted work without the owner mark (a loop's, a replay's, the clean room's) is not the owner's, and experiments cannot carry the mark. Only host code attaches these marks, as error values, never from a machine's, replay's or guest's text, so that text counts like any other. When both the evaluator's interruption and the context's cause are present, the cut is exempt only if both are marked; otherwise the unmarked one counts and is the one logged. Pressure, other revokes and any unknown cause count. A counted cut logs "a candidate run was cut short (`pressure` / `refused or revoked` / `unknown`); counted"; an exempt cut logs "a candidate run was cut short (its class); not counted" (counted per evaluation, whichever side was in flight). **Parking.** A candidate with `MaxExempt` (6) exempt cuts is parked: never struck, and no verdict. It is refused with `ErrParked`, before it spends a proposal ID or a save, unless the scheduler marks the evaluator idle (`WithIdle`, only in a pass where nothing but parked work was offered). Its loop is ordered last, so Loop 3's releases never wait behind it. Parking orders and idles: parking ends the pass, and its exempt count keeps growing. So `MaxExempt` bounds one pass, not one candidate. The count is per process (in memory, bounded to 1,024 candidates) and resets on restart. **Per case (PE5b, security B2 on #127):** an owner's cut of one (candidate, case) pair is exempt `MaxExemptPerCase` (2) times; the next counts under `MaxInterruptions` and logs `(<class>, past its exempt limit); counted`. Both counts per pair are saved in the state (`cuts`: counts and a time under `resumeKey`'s hash, no case content), so a restart resets neither; they expire with `ResumeFor`, are bounded to 1,024 pairs (oldest dropped), and go with the verdict. So owner cuts re-roll a case at most `MaxExemptPerCase` + `MaxInterruptions` times. **Idle turns:** the idle evaluator does not pick a parked candidate straight after its own idle run while another parked candidate has waited since; that one goes first. A candidate that waited once and was not offered again holds it back one idle pass at most. Its pairs stay bounded by `ResumeFor` and `MaxKeptPairs`. **Not yet:** counting pressure only when the candidate's machine is the largest consumer (needs per-machine usage). Nothing marks accepted work as the owner's yet, so `owner-work` is dormant until `Request.Owner` is set. | CHG-1, LOOP-10, RES-1 | A cause security finds a candidate can induce goes back to counting. |
+| C22 | **Kept pairs across nights (PE7 part 3).** `Config.ResumeFor` replaces the 12 h default where set; a box whose agent sleeps for learning sets 36 h, so an evaluation cut when one night's window ends resumes the next night (Loop 1's and Loop 2's kept candidates follow `LearnConfig.ResumeFor` and `GuardConfig.ResumeFor`). When the active tree changes (an adoption, an UNDO, a state reload), pairs kept against any other base are dropped at once. Until now they missed by key and waited to expire. PE5b's per-pair cut counts lapse on the same `ResumeFor` (L3 SHOULD-2 on #145 adds the expiry and cap tests). `KeptPairs` counts one candidate's kept pairs on the current base, and Loop 1 offers the hypothesis whose kept candidate has the most first. Pairs finished while the active tree moved on are not kept, and a parked candidate (PE5) counts no kept pairs, so it never leads Loop 1's order outside idle passes (L3 on #153). | LOOP-1, CHG-1 | `agentosd` sets the 36 h only where the agent sleeps for learning (daemon B17); elsewhere nothing changes. |
